    {
      "id": "midbj7e",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "PARLIAMENT OF INDIA"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "According to Article 79, Parliament consists of the President, Lok Sabha (House of the People), and Rajya Sabha (Council of States).",
        "Lok Sabha is directly elected for a 5-year term, whereas Rajya Sabha is a permanent house with one-third of its members retiring every two years.",
        "Money Bills can originate only in the Lok Sabha, and the decision of the Speaker regarding certification of a Money Bill is final.",
        "Joint Sessions are called to resolve legislative deadlocks and are presided over by the Speaker of Lok Sabha, but are not allowed for Money Bills or Constitutional Amendment Bills.",
        "The Anti-Defection Law was added via the 52nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1985, with the Speaker acting as the decision-making authority."
      ],
      "id": "wpqdobj"
    },
